Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Memory-context chunks as Sublet lifetimes
Upstream: postgresql 17.0 and 17.5
Build-Variants: Sublet replay and PGSU_NESTED=sublet
Depends-On: patches 0001 and 0002 of the memory-context component
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Every chunk a memory context hands out is a child lifetime (CDERIVE) of
its block's generation, bounded to MAXALIGN(size) bytes of the chunk's
payload, or one byte for an empty request. The chunk header lies outside
the caller's bounds. pfree and repalloc end the chunk (CREVOKE), so every
copy of the caller's pointer dies with it.

The chunk and block headers keep their stock layout. Every block holds as
many chunks as in stock, and the managers request the same blocks from
malloc. Each block's malloc reference and generation live in a block
table in mcxt.c. The table lies in static memory outside every block, and
entering a block calls no allocator. A two-level page map over the Sv39
user address space finds the block that holds an address. pfree,
repalloc, GetMemoryChunkContext and GetMemoryChunkSpace first load through
the caller's pointer, so a stale pointer faults (cause 25). They then find
the block by the pointer's address and read the chunk header through the
block's malloc reference. The allocator lends that reference only to a
pointer at the start of its chunk. An aligned redirect chunk, an address
in no block and a pointer into the middle of a chunk keep the caller's
own authority and nothing more.

A block derives its generation on first use. A reset that keeps the
block revokes the generation, which ends every chunk of the block, and
the next allocation derives a new one. Reset therefore takes no table
slot and cannot fail. Freeing a block removes it from the block table and
ends its malloc lifetime and every chunk below it.

CDERIVE returns an untagged 0 when the lifetime table has no free slot
and cannot grow, and the block table refuses a block when it is full.
The managers then give the chunk or block back through their own free
path (Bump rewinds its cursor) and report out of memory the way they do
for a failed malloc. Below 4 KiB the bounds are exact. Above, the bounds
format rounds both edges to a granule, and the managers keep their
placement. The child then also covers the granule slack around the
payload, inside its block's malloc object. For a dedicated block from
32 KiB on, that slack reaches into the block header. Bump has no chunk
header, so a Bump request for zero bytes takes one MAXALIGN unit.

Every realloc creates the new chunk before it ends the old one. The
in-place paths issue the new child, then revoke the old. The moving paths
allocate and copy first. An AllocSet single-chunk block uses malloc,
issue, copy and free instead of libc realloc, which would end the old
block before the new chunk exists. A failed realloc therefore leaves the
old chunk live and unchanged.

